@meta
id: 10-08
title: Drill — ci" on a config value
chapter: 10
type: drill
skills: obj-quote
par: 27
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: double-click, discover it only took one word, drag instead
open: database.json

@brief
Eight times: the database host should be `db.internal`, not the staging
one. Replace the value, save, quit.

The value has dots in it, so a double-click would only ever get you part
of it. The object does not care.

@keys
| `ci"` | change what is between the quotes |
| `f.` | a cheap way to land inside the value |

@hints
Get the cursor somewhere inside the value first — anywhere will do.
--
`ci"` then type the new host.

@solution
    2j f. ci" db.internal <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Twenty-three keystrokes, eleven of them the new host name.

The reason to drill this one rather than just read it: config files are
where you spend a surprising amount of your editing life, and they are
almost entirely strings. Once `ci"` is in your fingers you stop thinking
of a config value as text to be selected and start thinking of it as a
slot to be filled.

@start database.json
{
  "database": {
    "host": "db.staging.internal",
    "port": 5432,
    "pool": 10
  }
}

@expected database.json
{
  "database": {
    "host": "db.internal",
    "port": 5432,
    "pool": 10
  }
}
